A||||9||rogergarrison||Another Same Ole SUV Story Over Again||||11||
Z||000000||rogergarrison||12-22-2001||06:45 AM||||Dont take this as a SUV slam (even though I hate them myself). Another case of a 17 year old girl driving a SUV like its a Celica GT. Driving on the freeway just down the road from me, switching lanes to get ahead of everybody flipped it over. When are people going to learn to keep inexperienced drivers out of these kinds of vehicles. They have no concept of vehicle dynamics. Anyone with daughters or sons this young should think twice about getting them that cute little Rav4, Suzuki or Explorer. Older people have enough problems with them themselves. I drove a girlfriends Samari ONCE, and the way it drove scared the begibies out of me||||reg||11
Z||000001||LZeitgeist||12-22-2001||09:40 AM||||My recent ex has an Isuzu Rodeo, and she drives like a freaking maniac. Every time she would drive us *anywhere* (she smokes, so she ain't doing it the Formula!), I'd be scared sh!tless - she'd be driving 20 mph over the speed limit *ALL THE TIME*, EVERYWHERE, no matter what. A couple times I asked her if I could drive, and she said 'sure', and I'd mentioned that her driving made me nervous (nervous, hell - I was having to change my underwear every time we went somewhere), and she said that it was O.K., her friends told her the same thing.<P>(I thought, and I asked her why she didn't listen, then... she just blew it off as 'it's just how I drive'.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> )<P>Once when I asked for her keys so we could go somewhere, she said "you really hate my driving, don't you?" I calmly said, "Hon, your driving scares the holy living sh!t outta me." She got a little huffy and handed me the keys. While we were on the way, in order to hopefully soften the moment a bit, I gently told her that it really concerned me, and that riding motorcycles, I had learned that when I'm alone in/on a vehicle, my safety is in my hands, but when someone rides *with* me, *their* safety is in my hands, too, and I never, ever took that lightly. Also, that in driving Fieros (sometimes 'spiritedly') I could feel what the car was doing and handling during higher-performance driving, and that she was *always* right on the razor's edge of the vehicle's capabilities, and that most likely if *anything* unexpected happened anywhere around her, she'd be gone with no margin for error like that. <P>'Her other friends just hung on and prayed', she chuckled. I told her that I chose to take a more active role in my safety and well-being. She thought I was joking - I wasn't. <P>From then on, she'd just hand me her keys when we headed out together...<P>The only thing that pisses me off to no end is that when she finally goes, chances are vast that she's going to take someone else out with her. I certainly don't want her dead - not in any way, shape or form - but I don't see how she's avoided wrecks for as long as she has. She's got to have three guardian angels working overtime just to keep her alive in traffic. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||BN Boomer||12-22-2001||09:55 AM||||Oh brother. I drive my big honkin' 4x4 Suburban 20mph over the speed limit all the time. Back when Montana had no set speed limit, I would just set the cruise control at 98mph (where the speed limiter is set) and let 'er rip. Even now I routinely drive it at 80mph.<P>My daughter has been driving the Suburban occassionally since she was 16. I'd be a hell of a lot more worried about the average teenage boy in a V6 Fiero than I would about my daughter in our Suburban.<P>Blame the piss-poor drivers and the system that produces them, not the vehicles they operate.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Oreif||12-22-2001||10:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BN Boomer:<BR><B>Blame the piss-poor drivers and the system that produces them, not the vehicles they operate.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Agreed, They teach these "young adults" how to drive using Escorts and Cavaliers. They never teach them that different vehicles handle or need to be driven differently.<BR>They think that in a large SUV they are safer due to it's size. The same goes for high performance cars. In my opinion there is no reason for someone who has had their license less than a year, to be driving a 0-60 in less than 6 seconds car. Around here I see kids just get their licenses and their parents buy them a brand new Mustang GT/Tran Am/Import. Ususally the car is totalled within the first year. I've even seen a kid wipe out a 2 week old Grand Prix GTP. He got out of the car and told his friend not to worry, His Dad will just buy him another one! This was his second GTP in 3 months!!!<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif"><P>If it was my son, after the first one was totalled, he'd be riding a tri-cycle!! ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fformula88||12-22-2001||11:33 AM||||I cannot believe how some people drive their SUV's!  My father has a Geo Tracker convertable for use as a Winter vehicle, and that thing gets scary when driven hard!  Its just so high, that going fast around corners makes it feel very tippy.  It does have its good attributes too, 8 inches of ground clearance for Buffalo snow storms, and the convertable top is fun in the summer!  But a sports car it certainly is NOT.  Leave the high G turns to the Fiero's of the world!||||reg||1
Z||000005||ITB 2M4||12-22-2001||12:04 PM||||More needs to be done on the driver education part, rather than placing blame on an inanimate object.  An SUV without someone behind the wheel is inherently stable.  Anyone one remember G.I.G.O.?<P>When I took Driver's Ed. in high school, my instructor was the basketball coach.  He would get in the car with the sports section, tune the radio to Dallas Cowboys Weekly, and start reading.  He never looked at anything but that paper the whole time!  Luckily for me, my dad had been involved in SCCA since '70, and when I turned 16, we went to the auto-x every month.  I learned more in one weekend of auto-x'ing than in 6months of Driver's Ed.!<P>If you've got a teen, the best thing you can do for them is to either take them to the auto-x, or get them a driver's training school at someplace like Skip Barber or Bondaraunt.  Bertil Roos' school has an excellent skid recovery class for those of you in the places where it ices over.<P>BTW, the lead story on the noon news here right now, is a kid that was street racing his Mustang, and ran into a car that pulled out in front of him, killing the driver of that car.  Turns out, it was his mom......<BR>SUV's aren't the problem.....<P>------------------<BR>SCCA ITB '84 Fiero 2M4<BR>AOL IM- DGPFiero||||reg||14
Z||000006||BlackStang95||12-22-2001||12:14 PM||||"My daughter has been driving the Suburban occassionally since she was 16. I'd be a hell of a lot more worried about the average teenage boy in a V6 Fiero than I would about my daughter in our Suburban."<P><BR>I couldn't agree more.  Spektrum drove his 91 cherokee for about a year, I seen him top out the speedometer (90 or so) and speed often, nothing even close to a wreck.  He had owned his fiero gt 2 months and well, search for the thread "Fiero Crash" for more info.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||12-22-2001||06:06 PM||||latest update on this one. The 17 yo girl died today, it was a Blazer.||||reg||1
Z||000008||LZeitgeist||12-22-2001||06:22 PM||||Just for clarification to what I mentioned above - I'm 33, she's 38. She *definitely* should have learned better by now... and hey, 20 mph over the speed limit on the highway doesn't bother me anywhere *near* as much as 20 over through neighborhoods, main roads, thoroughfares... *everywhere*. ::shakes head:: I won't be missing *that* facet of the relationship...||||reg||1
Z||000009||BN Boomer||12-22-2001||06:58 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR>[B]Just for clarification to what I 20 mph over the speed limit on the highway doesn't bother me anywhere *near* as much as 20 over through neighborhoods, main roads, thoroughfares... *everywhere*.[B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I agree, but if some arshole comes ripping through my neighborhood doing 20mph over the speed limit, it doesn't matter if he/she/it is driving an SUV, a Fiero, or a Ferrari for that matter. I catch them doing it and find out where they live or work, they might come out to find thier valve stems missing.<BR>||||reg||1
